            {
              "type": "text",
              "id": "goJBP",
              "name": "Diff Text",
              "fill": "$color.text.primary",
              "textGrowth": "fixed-width",
              "width": "fill_container",
              "content": "Three attacks in three weeks. Each failure weighed on him, stones stacked in his chest. Kellan scraped dried tallow from the lip of the lantern with a split thumbnail. The silence in the cellar was worse than the gunfire had been; it gave the dead room to breathe.\n\nThe first strike at the canal sluice had been sloppy—an ill-timed fuse that did little more than scorch the limestone and alert the garrison. The second, three nights later along the perimeter wall, had cost him four men and their only decent long-rifle when a calcium flare caught them against the pale brick.\n\nBut tonight at the railyard had severed bone. They hadn’t even reached the switchline before the ironclad patrol rolled out of the fog, searchlights blazing, machine guns already primed.",
              "lineHeight": 1.6,
              "fontFamily": "$font.sans",
              "fontSize": "$font.size.body",
              "fontWeight": "normal"
            }
          ]
        },
